<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick Christian, Elector of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1722">1722</a>–<a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick Christian was Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> for about ten weeks in 1763, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. With his advisers he began the Rétablissement, a programme of financial and economic reform to rebuild the war-ruined electorate, but he died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in December 1763. His brother Prince Xavier then governed as regent for his young son, Frederick Augustus III.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-christian-elector-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Frederick Christian, Elector of Saxony" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pietro Rotari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Christian_by_Pietro_Rotari_(altered_and_edited).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick Christian was born on 5 September 1722, the third but eldest surviving son of Frederick Augustus II, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and Maria Josepha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He was a weak child, suffered paralysis in one foot and depended on a wheelchair early in life. His mother repeatedly tried to induce him to take monastic vows and give up his rights of succession to his younger brothers, but he refused. The early deaths of his two older brothers, one stillborn in 1721 and Joseph Augustus in 1728, made him heir. On 13 June 1747 he married his cousin Maria Antonia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> by proxy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, and again in person at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 20 June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Frederick Christian succeeded his father on 5 October 1763. He had already written in his diary that princes exist for their subjects, not subjects for their princes, and that his subjects' wealth, public credit and a well-standing army made up a prince's true happiness, which showed his openness to the ideas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. One of his first acts was to dismiss the unpopular prime minister Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-bruhl">Heinrich von Brühl</a>, whose failed economic policy and catastrophic foreign policy had drawn the electorate into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>.</p><p>He began to rebuild the wrecked finances of the plundered and devastated country through reforms known as the Rétablissements, which pared down court expenses and simplified administration on principles of economy. Most members of his government, such as Thomas von Fritsch, Friedrich Ludwig Wurmb and Christian Gotthelf Gutschmied, were of middle-class origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frederick Christian died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> on 17 December 1763 and was buried in the Hofkirche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>. His eldest son, Frederick Augustus III, was a minor, so the electorate was governed jointly by the boy's uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> and the Dowager Electress Maria Antonia until his majority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick Christian, Elector of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Seven Years’ War the Rétablissement, begun under <b>Frederick Christian</b> and continued under Frederick Augustus III, reduced debt, reformed finances and encouraged industry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1748" aria-label="Dresden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4862 394 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick Christian, Elector of Saxony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5312 664h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1748">What was here in 1748? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
